# MD AI reverse proxy — docs/architecture/08-deployment-architecture.md §3-4.
#
# Default mode: Cloudflare Tunnel. `cloudflared` (run separately, outside
# this compose file — it needs a real per-deployment tunnel token) makes an
# outbound-only connection to Cloudflare and forwards to this Caddy instance
# on plain HTTP; Cloudflare terminates TLS at the edge, so no inbound port
# needs to be opened on the Oracle instance at all. That's why this listens
# on :80, not :443, and has no `tls` directive — Caddy is only ever reached
# over localhost/the tunnel, never directly from the internet in this mode.
#
# Direct-exposure mode (not the default — see §4 for why): replace the
# `:80 {` line below with your real domain (`your-domain.example.com {`)
# and add a `tls your@email.example.com` directive; Caddy will then obtain
# and renew a Let's Encrypt certificate automatically. This requires a real
# public DNS record and an open :443 on the instance's security list/NSG,
# which is exactly the exposure Tunnel mode is designed to avoid.
#
# WireGuard mode: no reverse proxy is needed at all — the phone reaches
# `backend` directly over the VPN interface. Don't run this Caddy service
# in that mode.

:80 {
	reverse_proxy backend:8080
}
